%% This file contains examples of some of the input and editing features of 
WinScore. To see the output from these examples, start WSCORE.EXE and 
type READ GETTING STARTED. The first four lines (8 staves) of Mozart's 
Piano Sonata, K.545, will appear on the screen. Study this file to see how the 
commands produce the graphics results.

If the percent sign, %, appears as the first character in a line, the line is
considered as a comment. If two percent signs, %%, appear, then all the lines
until the next appearance of %% will be taken as comment lines (as is the case
with these lines.)

The basic group input command starts with:
   IN a b c d e
a = staff number  Staves are numbered from 1 to 256 up from the bottom.
b = left end position of staff - can be from 0 to 200.
c = vertical position offset from "normal" in scale steps.
    In general, the vertical position of staff 1 should not be altered.
d = staff size factor. This factor controls the size of all items 
    attached to this staff.
e = right end position of staff - 0-200. 
    If e is left at 0, it assumes 200, as in these examples.
If no staff "a" is present, a new one is created with the given parameters. 
The standard staff length is 7 1/2 inches. For editing purposes, this is 
divided into 200 points. (However, fractional horizontal positions may be 
used, e.g. 47.63, etc.)

The next input line, 0 200, sets boundries for the current input.

Following is the 5-pass input procedure.

The pass one contains code for notes, rests, measure lines, key signatures,
meter information. The slash separates the items. The input must end
with a semicolon. (A second line of notes, etc. may be entered if the first
line ends with a slash.)
M2/ = measure line (bar line) covering 2 staves up. In general, it is best
     to enter the bottom staff of a system first. You will not need to
     enter bar lines on the upper staves. The number need not be repeated
     for ensuing bar lines.
TR/ = treble clef. Other clef codes are AL/ = alto, TE/ = tenor, BA/ = bass,
     PR/ = percussion.
COM/ = common time (C). CUT/ = cut time (alla breve). Any number pairs, 3 4/,
     4 4/, 6 8/ etc. may be entered.
Notes are entered with their conventional letter names, C,D,E,F,G,A,B. Accidentals
are entered as F = flat, S = sharp, N = natural, FF = double flat, SS = double sharp.
The octave levels are given as 0 through 8. A0 is the lowest A on the piano keyboard,
C1 is the lowest C, C2 is the low C of the cello, C4 is middle C, C is the highest C
on the piano. The octave number persists until a new number is given. However if "P"
(for proximity mode), precedes the note letter, then new octave numbers are created 
internally to maintain the smallest interval between two notes. For example, C4/B/
produces a major 7th skip from middle C up to B. However PC4/B/ produces a minor
2nd, as if you typed C4/B3/. Care should be taken to distinguish between intervals 
of the 5th and 4th. P persists until an "O" appears. OC, etc. (O = ordinary) turns 
off proximity mode. 

R = rest (the rhythmic value is given in the next line.)
See the online help for further details.
 
